From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] kbuild: dead code elimination without -fdata-sections

CONFIG_LD_DEAD_CODE_DATA_ELIMINATION compiles with -ffunction-sections
-fdata-sections. With -fdata-sections GCC 16 generates different code
for functions that address static data: 22 of the hot functions changed
size. With every one of them still at its measured offset, a pipe round
trip between two processes took 17 % more cycles (I-cache misses 81 to
99 per round trip) and sched_yield() had twice the I-cache misses.

Keep -ffunction-sections and --gc-sections, and leave the data in its
per-object sections: unreferenced functions are still discarded. The
data is kept whole anyway, at fixed D-cache offsets
(arch/riscv/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S).

---
diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -1076,5 +1076,8 @@
 # `rustc`'s `-Zfunction-sections` applies to data too (as of 1.59.0).
 ifdef CONFIG_LD_DEAD_CODE_DATA_ELIMINATION
-KBUILD_CFLAGS_KERNEL += -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections
+# No -fdata-sections: it changes the code GCC generates for functions that
+# address static data, and the hot paths with it
+# (arch/riscv/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S).
+KBUILD_CFLAGS_KERNEL += -ffunction-sections
 KBUILD_RUSTFLAGS_KERNEL += -Zfunction-sections=y
 LDFLAGS_vmlinux += --gc-sections
